      <EssayHeader slug="ask-who-the-party-secretary-is" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> Anyone doing business with a Chinese company should ask in the first meeting who its Communist Party secretary is and what its charter lets him decide, because Chinese law and practice put the Party and the state ahead of your contract, and all of it is published.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">There is a question that belongs in the first meeting with any Chinese counterparty. It is almost never asked until the third year, usually by a lawyer, usually after something has gone wrong. The question is: who is your Party secretary, and what does your charter say he is entitled to decide?</p>
        <p>It sounds impolite. It is not. It is closer to asking who chairs the audit committee. The structure being asked about is a legally mandated body of the enterprise, disclosed in filings, and the counterparty’s compliance department can produce the answer before lunch. The discomfort Western executives feel comes from projecting their own vocabulary onto a system that uses different words and conceals far less than they assume. It publishes. We decline to read.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-03-060044/region-45s-80e/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1152"
          height="896"
          alt="Painting of biomorphic forms on cream, dominated by a large open eye in orange and black"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          45S 80E, Southern Indian Ocean, rendered from that day’s atmospheric data.
          <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-03-060044/region-45s-80e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70">View the painting</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Say the Quiet Part</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Begin with something most institutions will say privately and almost none will put in a memo. The People’s Republic under the Communist Party is a strategic adversary of the United States and its allies. It says so in its own doctrinal language, and the commercial relationship is an instrument of that competition, not an exception to it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">This is not a mood. It is the working assumption that makes the observable facts fit together. The alternative assumption, that we are dealing with a normal counterparty who occasionally behaves strangely, requires explaining away a decade of consistent behavior as a series of unfortunate coincidences.</p>
        <p>The category error runs deep in Western boardrooms. The last forty years trained a generation of executives to treat market access as the ultimate goal and everything else as friction to be handled by government affairs. That training produced enormous returns, and it is now a liability, because the counterparty never accepted the frame. It was always playing a longer game with different objectives, and it wrote them down.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Doctrine Is Published</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Military-civil fusion is not an allegation made by hawks. It is a stated national strategy, elevated under the current leadership and run through a central commission set up for the purpose. Its entire point is to erase the separation between commercial technology development and military capability. When a Western firm partners with a Chinese entity on advanced technology, whether the result reaches the People’s Liberation Army is not a matter of the partner’s good intentions. It is a matter of published policy.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The industrial roadmap issued in 2015 (Made in China 2025) named the sectors in which domestic champions were to displace foreign suppliers, with target percentages. The document was later played down in propaganda after it drew attention abroad, but the substance continued. Firms in every named sector went through the same sequence: welcome, joint venture, technology transfer as a condition of access, domestic competitor, shrinking margins, exit.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The theft cases are documented in Western courts, not just in think tank papers.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A Chinese wind turbine manufacturer was convicted in a U.S. federal court in 2018 of stealing the source code of its American supplier, a theft that gutted the victim’s market value.</li>
          <li>A Taiwanese chipmaker pleaded guilty in 2020 in connection with the misappropriation of an American memory manufacturer’s trade secrets, destined for a mainland partner.</li>
          <li>A 2018 indictment of hackers associated with the Ministry of State Security described a campaign dating to 2006 that included, from 2014, intrusions into managed service providers, the vendors holding the keys to everyone else’s networks.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>None of this is contested by anyone who reads the filings. It is merely inconvenient for the deal in front of you.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Law Does Not Recognize Your Contract</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The legal architecture is the part that turns geopolitics into a term you can underwrite.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">A national intelligence law enacted in 2017 requires organizations and citizens to support and cooperate with intelligence work, with a legal basis for compelling assistance. Data security and personal information laws passed in 2021 subject data generated in the country to state access and restrict its export. The counter-espionage law was broadened in 2023 to expand what may be treated as protected material, which is to say it expanded the state’s discretion.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Two consequences follow for anyone signing anything.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>First, your counterparty’s obligations to the state are not contractual, so they cannot be contracted away.</strong> A confidentiality agreement allocates risk between two commercial parties. It does not stand against a lawful instruction from a state that does not recognize your agreement as a competing claim.</p>
        <p><strong>Second, when the instruction comes, it will be lawful where it is issued.</strong> Your counterparty will not have betrayed you in his own frame of reference. He will have complied. If you have filed this under bad faith, you will misread every signal until the morning it happens, because you will be watching for a liar and will meet a man discharging an obligation you declined to put on your risk register.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Coercion Is an Instrument, Not an Accident</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The pattern that should end the debate about intent is what happens to countries that displease the Party.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Australia</strong> called for an independent inquiry into the origins of the pandemic in 2020 and found its barley, wine, coal and other exports hit by a rolling campaign of tariffs and informal bans.</li>
          <li><strong>Lithuania</strong> allowed a Taiwanese representative office to open under that name in 2021 and found that its goods, and eventually other countries’ goods containing Lithuanian components, could not clear customs.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">Neither episode was a trade dispute in the ordinary sense. Both involved a state using the commercial relationship as a lever against the political choices of a sovereign government, which is exactly what the relationship is for.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Export controls run the same direction. Restrictions announced in 2023 on gallium, germanium, graphite and rare earth processing technology were not neutral industrial policy. They were a demonstration that dependence is a weapon that can be fired, by the state that had spent two decades acquiring the position.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Then there are people. Two Canadian citizens were detained in December 2018, days after Canada arrested a Chinese executive on a U.S. warrant, and were released in September 2021 within hours of that executive’s release. Whatever one calls that, it is not a legal system a foreign firm should expect to treat its own employees predictably when the wind changes. Exit bans on foreign executives in commercial disputes are documented practice.</p>
        <p>And for anyone who believes a signed instrument constrains the outcome, consider the treaty governing Hong Kong’s handover, registered with the United Nations. A Foreign Ministry spokesman described it in 2017 as a historical document without practical significance. Three years later the national security law arrived, a free press was dismantled, and the territory’s most prominent newspaper publisher went to prison. That is the precedent that matters for underwriting. The point is not that a treaty was broken, but that its breach was announced calmly, in advance, and the counterparties who had priced it as binding simply absorbed the loss.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Ownership Was Always Conditional</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The commercial cases are ownership stories rather than espionage stories, which is why they generalize.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An insurance conglomerate ran one of the more aggressive cross-border acquisition programs of the last decade. Its chairman was sentenced to eighteen years in 2018 and the state took the company.</li>
          <li>A private investment group of comparable scale lost its founder, who was removed from Hong Kong in 2017 and sentenced on the mainland in 2022, and its assets were disposed of by the authorities.</li>
          <li>A payments giant, days from the largest stock listing in history, had it halted in November 2020 and spent years being restructured.</li>
          <li>A ride-hailing company listed in New York in mid-2021 was placed under cybersecurity review within days, and was delisted within a year.</li>
          <li>A prominent technology banker disappeared in early 2023. His firm later confirmed he was assisting an investigation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">Every one of these looked privately owned on any Western balance sheet. In every one, the residual claimant, the party that ultimately owned what was left, turned out to be the state.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Now consider the structure through which most foreign investors held these companies. The variable interest entity (VIE) was a workaround for bans on foreign ownership in restricted sectors. What the offshore shareholder owns is a Cayman Islands company holding contracts that purport to capture the profits of a mainland operating company it does not own. The arrangement has never been affirmatively approved in a way that would survive a determined adverse ruling. Millions of Western retirement accounts hold this instrument. Most of the people who own it believe they own shares in a Chinese company. They own a contract, governed in part by a legal system that has shown us what it thinks of contracts that become inconvenient.</p>
        <p>Audit access followed the same shape. American law finally forced the issue of inspecting the auditors of China-based companies listed in the U.S., and access was granted in late 2022 after years of refusal. “Granted” is the operative word. What was extended under pressure can be withdrawn under different pressure. And the underlying position, that the books of these companies were beyond the reach of the regulator whose market they were listed in, was held for over a decade without embarrassment.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-02-060044/tropical-south-america-15s-70w/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1152"
          height="896"
          alt="Painting of overlapping rounded forms in pink, grey and ochre, layered like stacked shells"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
