          15S 70W, Tropical South America, rendered from that day’s atmospheric data.
          <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-02-060044/tropical-south-america-15s-70w/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70">View the painting</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Target Is the Party-State, Not the People</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">One distinction is worth stating precisely, and not as a hedge.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The Party’s most effective rhetorical move is to claim that it is China, so that criticism of a political apparatus reads as hostility toward a civilization of 1.4 billion people. Accepting that conflation does its work for it. It is also strategically stupid, because it gives up the strongest fact available: the system falls hardest on Chinese citizens. The founders in the cases above were Chinese. The entrepreneurs who lost a decade to an unappealable decision were Chinese. The people in Xinjiang who are the subject of American import restrictions are Chinese. A hawk who cannot tell the Politburo from a Chinese engineer in Shenzhen is not a hardliner. He is a bad analyst.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">It is also poor tradecraft. Screening by ancestry generates noise, is unlawful as an employment practice in most jurisdictions where your firm operates, and misses the real mechanism, which is structural and documented. The American research-security effort that drifted in that direction produced collapsed prosecutions and ruined careers, while the real transfers continued through channels that were, the whole time, described in public filings.</p>
        <p>Aim at the institution. It is a larger target and it does not move.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Symmetry Argument, and Why It Fails</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Someone in the meeting always observes that Western states also lean on their firms: cleared contractors under mitigation agreements, export controls that conscript compliance departments, sanctions regimes that deputize banks, officials cycling in and out of industry. The observation is true, and it is not equivalence.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The difference is contestability. In the American or European case the instruction arrives through a process that can be read, challenged, litigated and occasionally defeated, and the record survives. A firm can refuse and go to court. The relevant question for underwriting is not whether a state can reach into an enterprise, since all of them can. It is whether the reaching leaves a record the counterparty can see, contest and price.</p>
        <p>Where it does not, you are not holding a contract. You are holding a permission that has not yet been revoked.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">What to Actually Ask</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The practical version fits on one page and belongs in the first management meeting, not the final confirmation phase.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Does the entity have a Party committee, and is it named in the articles of association?</li>
          <li>Who is the secretary, and does he hold a board or executive title?</li>
          <li>Does the charter require the Party committee to discuss matters before board resolutions, and on which categories?</li>
          <li>What is the ownership chain up to the ultimate beneficial owner, including any state asset supervision body, provincial vehicle or government guidance fund?</li>
          <li>Are there special shares held by state-linked entities, and what consent rights attach to them?</li>
          <li>Where does operational data reside, which laws govern its export, and what happens to our access on a bad day?</li>
          <li>Which of our people could be prevented from leaving the country if a dispute escalated?</li>
          <li>Does any part of the supply chain touch Xinjiang, and can you document it to a standard that survives a customs presumption of forced labor?</li>
          <li>If a lawful instruction conflicted with our agreement, who makes that call, and would we be told?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The last question is the only uncomfortable one. It is uncomfortable because it asks a man to describe, in front of you, the circumstances under which he will lawfully defect. Serious operators answer it. They have thought about it more carefully than you have, because they live inside it.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Variance Is the Finding</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Ask the full list of three people at the same organization, on separate occasions.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">A competent counterparty answers within a day, because the answers are on file and the structure is legally required to exist. Nothing has been extracted. You have read the organization chart aloud.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Evasion is therefore informative, and it comes in grades. Puzzlement is usually genuine and means you are below the level where the question is real. Offense is a negotiating posture. Assurance without documents, especially the assurance that the committee is a pure formality, is the one to record word for word and date, because a formality nobody will describe in writing is not a formality.</p>
        <p>The pattern to price is inconsistency. Three different accounts of who must be consulted before a major decision are not evidence of conspiracy. They are evidence that nobody in the room holds authority he can guarantee to you, which is more mundane and far more expensive.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Some Exposures Should Not Be Priced</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Most of this can be managed with structure. Keep the crown jewels out of a single jurisdiction. Assume any process that can be observed will be reproduced, and decide in advance whether you can survive that. Use a second source for anything whose interruption stops the line. Know which of your people carry exit risk, and stop sending them casually. Put forced transfer in the model as a branch with a probability, rather than a footnote about “geopolitical uncertainty,” which is the phrase people use when they intend to think no further.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">But the harder discipline is knowing which exposures do not belong in the model at all.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A supply chain that cannot be documented clear of forced labor is not a discount-rate problem. It is a line you decline to cross, and U.S. law now agrees with that instinct by presuming the worst until you prove otherwise.</li>
          <li>Technology whose transfer measurably advances the military capability of an adversary is not a margin question.</li>
          <li>Dependence on a single hostile jurisdiction for an input you cannot substitute is not a risk you hedge. It is a position you unwind, slowly and expensively, starting before you are forced to.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">The refusal to ask is not politeness. It is a decision to hold an unpriced position in something you have chosen not to examine, made by people who would never accept the same treatment of a covenant, a lien or a tax exposure.</p>
        <p>Ask in the first meeting, when it is diligence. In the fourth year, after the joint venture is signed, the technology has moved and your engineering lead has been hired away, it is an accusation, and you will be making it with no leverage and no room to maneuver.</p>
      </div>
